WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.870
Good morning, Hank. It's Friday, January 21,
2011. I want to continue our discussion about

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.940
how the truth resists simplicity by talking
about narrative complexity, but in order to

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.049
do that I need to talk about my favorite person:
ME! Okay, Hank. So the biggest award in young

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:13.769
adult literature is called the Printz Award,
which looks like this (award stamp on Looking

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:16.280
for Alaska) on a book, and looks like this
(actual Printz Award) in a basement. And the

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.150
single most important moment in my whole professional
life happened five years ago, almost to the

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:24.779
day, when I received the call saying that
Looking for Alaska, my first novel, had won

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:28.650
the Printz Award. And it was especially meaningful
to me because my first job after college was

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.980
as a temp at the magazine, Booklist. And on
my first day at Booklist, I had to retype,

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:39.399
that's how old I am, all these speeches that
were given at the first Printz Award reception.

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:43.520
And that is the good narrative, right? You
start out with this twenty-two year old professional

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.440
data entrist who writes for years and years
at night and on the weekends, and then eventually

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.240
it comes out and he wins the very award that
he used to type the speeches for. And you

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.739
can imagine this young writer working in isolation
and getting rejected over and over and over

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:01.360
again until a publisher takes a chance on
him. But Hank, the actual story of how this

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.229
book came to exist and have this shiny piece
of gold on it is different and more complicated

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:07.430
so I thought I would try to tell that story
today.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.820
So I did write this story at night and on
the weekends for three years, but I had constant

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.560
help from my friend, Ilene Cooper, who was
one of my bosses at Booklist and who's also

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.570
an author. Ilene probably read and commented
on like a dozen drafts of Looking for Alaska--which

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.150
by the way, wasn't even called Looking for
Alaska until my friend, Keir Graff came up

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.500
with that title. Then, once Ilene was happy
and I paid Keir his two dollars for coming

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.579
up with the title, Ilene and I sent it to
Penguin and they agreed to publish it, provided

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:33.710
I revised extensively. Although, I had no
idea about how extensively I would have to

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:34.710
revise it.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.840
So this book, for those of you who haven't
read it, is about a guy who memorizes the

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.939
last words of famous people, and falls in
love with a girl named Alaska. Only, when

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:45.100
Penguin agreed to buy the book, it wasn't
about a guy who memorizes the last words of

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.380
famous people. In fact, a lot of what people
like about the book, from the "Great Perhaps"

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.719
to the labyrinth, came out in those revisions
after I'd sold the book to Penguin. So Hank,

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:55.549
about four years after I started, I had a
reasonably finished manuscript. Although,

00:01:55.549 --> 00:01:59.250
it would be wrong to say that I wrote that
book. The truth is that Ilene wrote parts

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.340
of it, and my editor, Julie Strauss-Gabel
wrote parts of it, and my wife wrote parts

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.560
of it. In fact, all these people wrote at
least a little bit of Looking for Alaska.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.610
And I am grateful to each and every one of
those people, although no, they cannot have

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.610
any of my royalties.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.950
We all like this idea that a book is created
by a single person for a single reader because

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.610
it's a narrative that makes sense, but the
truth resists that simplicity. As the great

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.640
Carl Sagan said, "If you wish to make an apple
pie from scratch, you must first invent the

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.640
universe."

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.090
So all these people worked to make a book.
It came out. Twelve people in a room decided

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.630
this award (Printz), and I was really happy.
And then a little part of me was like, so

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:37.040
what? What I eventually realized is that the
real business of books is not done by awards

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.810
committees, or people who turn trees into
paper, or editors, or agents, or even writers--we're

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.001
all just facilitators. The real business is
done by readers. And the Looking for Alaska

00:02:46.001 --> 00:02:50.610
that you read is not quite like the Looking
for Alaska that anyone else reads. The hundreds

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.980
of thousands of Looking for Alaska readers'
stories are not something I can hold in my

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.670
hand or even get my head around.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.530
But Hank, I do want to say to you and all
the Nerdfighters who've read my books, that

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.730
being able to participate in big, interesting
conversations with you through stories like

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.060
this one (Looking for Alaska) and my other
books is really the most fulfilling thing

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.150
about my life. Just kidding! It's Henry and
Sarah! But you guys are great too. Seriously,

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:16.120
if I didn't have such a cute kid and a great
wife, you would be at the top. Well, pizza.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:22.630
It goes Sarah and Henry, pizza, the rest of
my family, Nerdfighteria, The Mountain Goats--that's

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:28.240
my top five. Hank! I am Freedom in Panem,
and I will see you Friday (rolls up to look

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.350
at calendar). It is Friday! I'm so bad at
that that! (while spinning around in chair

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:31.870
and flailing arms in agony)

